</blockquote>
<p>Now, if the italics throughout didn’t imply that we’re supposed to read this with an undercurrent of eye-roll, then punctuating the whole thing with a ‘Meh’ got the tone across loud and clear.</p>
<p>That’s why we wanted to go through and address these concerns one at a time.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, two power banks which, allowing for wireless charging losses, will probably <em>just barely</em> bring a typical phone up from 10% to a full charge…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yep.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>…<em>once</em>…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>…before they need to be charged themselves.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Uh-huh.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And that’s <em>if</em> they still have a full charge when they’re pulled out of the pocket or backpack or purse where they’ve sat, half forgotten.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Correct.</p>
<p>Sorry, we’re being a little facetious. But you see, the complaint laid out here… is actually just the product’s sales pitch.</p>
<p>No, they’re not power stations. Each one is not capable of recharging your phone over and over again. So if you’re looking for something to get through a power outage, look elsewhere. (Or, even better, keep looking here every day; we sell those sometimes.)</p>
<p>But 10% to full, at a pretty high speed? That could be the difference between a public transit commute spent enjoying an insightful podcast and texting your friends, or one with a dead phone, leaving you with nothing to do but listen to both sides of your seatmate’s conversation with their friend (because, for some reason, they’re video-chatting without earbuds in public) that begins with the phrase: “My dermatologist is back on their bullshit again.”</p>
<p>Or, to put it differently, haven’t you ever been in a situation where you would pay $25 for a single recharge? Well, these can each do that every day, without taking up a bunch of space in your bag. Assuming they’re charged, that is.</p>
<p>That is one thing we’ll give the commenter: they do need to be charged to work.</p>